WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.079
Default credentials, sample accounts, and vendor defaults are common attack targets.

2
00:00:05.079 --> 00:00:14.079
Any default identity, token, password, or administrative path should be removed, changed, or tightly controlled before production use.

3
00:00:14.079 --> 00:00:22.639
Open cloud storage and permissive access policies can expose customer files, backups, logs, exports, or build artifacts.

4
00:00:22.639 --> 00:00:26.651
Cloud configuration is part of the application security boundary.

5
00:00:26.651 --> 00:00:29.514
Unnecessary services increase attack surface.

6
00:00:29.514 --> 00:00:37.447
Every open port, enabled plugin, feature flag, debug route, and administrative function should have a documented purpose and owner.

7
00:00:37.447 --> 00:00:45.289
Debug configurations can disclose stack traces, secrets, paths, environment variables, and internal architecture.

8
00:00:45.289 --> 00:00:49.899
Diagnostic detail belongs in protected logs rather than public responses.

9
00:00:49.899 --> 00:00:55.556
Use secure baselines for applications, systems, containers, and cloud services.

10
00:00:55.556 --> 00:00:58.470
Harden configurations before production release.

11
00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:02.750
Manage configuration changes through reviewed and repeatable processes.

12
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.891
Selected SOC 2 mappings for this topic include CC7.

13
00:01:06.891 --> 00:01:10.922
1 - Vulnerability and configuration management; CC8.

14
00:01:10.922 --> 00:01:12.808
1 - Change control procedures.
